WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.940
Python provides vetted tempfile abstractions because temporary file
creation is more subtle than choosing a convenient name.

2
00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:16.450
At a high level, the tempfile module can create temporary files, temporary
directories, named files, and automatically managed objects with generated
unpredictable names.

3
00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:22.425
These APIs exist to avoid common mistakes around collisions, permissions,
cleanup, and shared directories.

4
00:00:22.425 --> 00:00:27.116
They should usually be preferred over handcrafted filenames in a temporary
location.

5
00:00:27.116 --> 00:00:30.393
Context-managed cleanup is one of the most useful patterns.

6
00:00:30.393 --> 00:00:38.425
A context manager can create a temporary file or directory for a bounded
block of work and remove it when the block ends, including many failure
cases.

7
00:00:38.425 --> 00:00:41.124
That does not remove the need for design judgment.

8
00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:52.240
The application still needs to decide where temporary storage should live,
what permissions are appropriate, how much data may be written, whether
the data is sensitive, and what happens if cleanup fails.

9
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.489
Choosing the right abstraction matters.

10
00:00:54.489 --> 00:00:59.308
A temporary file may fit when a single stream of data needs to be passed
to a library or tool.

11
00:00:59.308 --> 00:01:06.955
A temporary directory may fit when a workflow needs multiple related
files, extracted archive contents, or separate input and output paths.

12
00:01:06.955 --> 00:01:10.103
Text and binary modes should match the data being handled.

13
00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:17.750
Automatically managed unnamed files may be useful for some workflows,
while named files may be required when another component needs a path.

14
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.999
Safe defaults still require careful use.

15
00:01:19.999 --> 00:01:36.191
A temporary file API can generate a safer name, but it cannot decide
whether a user is authorized to process the data, whether sensitive
content should be written to disk, whether a subprocess should receive the
path, whether logs should include metadata, or how long a report should
remain available.

16
00:01:36.191 --> 00:01:42.360
Vetted APIs reduce dangerous mechanics, while application design controls
trust, sensitivity, and lifecycle.
